---
description: How to add or update a client in clients.json so one deployment serves per-client name, address and phone
alwaysApply: true
---

# Adding a client

A client entry holds three fields, all optional except `name`:

```json
"gurugram-landbase": {
  "name": "Gurugram Landbase",
  "address": "Plot No. 94, Sector 110, Dwarka Expressway, Gurugram 122017",
  "phone": "+919792069206"
}
```

## Trigger 1 — a pasted spreadsheet row

When the user pastes a tab-separated row, for example:

```
Gurugram Landbase	Plot No. 94, Sector 110, Dwarka Expressway, Gurugram 122017	9792069206	Gurgaon	Yes	Grotal	2026-10-01	...	Sent
```

read only the first three tab-separated columns and ignore every other column:

| Column | Field |
| --- | --- |
| 1 | `name` |
| 2 | `address` |
| 3 | `phone` |

## Trigger 2 — a spoken instruction

"Add client: `<Company Name>`, id `<client-id>`" — use the given id and name, and
leave `address` and `phone` out unless the user supplied them.

## Rules

1. **Derive the id from the name**: lowercase, non-alphanumerics collapsed to
   single hyphens, no leading or trailing hyphen. "Gurugram Landbase" becomes
   `gurugram-landbase`. An explicitly given id wins over the derived one.
2. **Format the phone as `+91XXXXXXXXXX`**: strip spaces, dashes and brackets,
   keep the last 10 digits, prefix `+91`. If fewer than 10 digits survive, treat
   the phone as missing.
3. **Update, never duplicate**: if the id already exists in `clients.json`,
   overwrite that entry's fields in place.
4. **Never borrow another client's details.** A missing address or phone stays
   out of the JSON; the site hides those elements rather than falling back to the
   default client's. Writing a placeholder number would make the Call button
   dial someone else's business.
5. **Only edit `clients.json`.** No other file, and no other entry — including
   `default`.
6. Commit that one file, push to `main`, and print the finished link:

```
https://gurgaon-realestate-prototype.teamrsdigitalgroup.workers.dev/?c=<id>
```

## How it works

`clients.json` is imported at build time by `lib/clients.ts`. Every page reads
the `c` query parameter server-side through `resolveBroker` in `lib/broker.ts`,
so the details are in the first byte of HTML and never flash the previous
client's. The resolved name, address and phone feed the nav, hero, footer,
contact section, property pages, `<title>` and the meta description from that one
source.

A missing or unknown `c` falls back to the `default` entry, so the plain link
with no query string keeps working exactly as before.

`https://<worker>/clients.json` shows which entries made it into the live bundle.
